Chapter: KeysCollision
  cites UI chapter KeyInput
  cites Foreword chapter CCE

 The test that would have caught it. `poll-key` used to answer named keys
 with DOM key codes while answering characters with ASCII, and DOM key codes
 land on printable characters, so Up and shift+7 were both 38 and Right and
 the apostrophe were both 39. Every existing keyboard test typed one side or
 the other. None typed both, so the suite could not express the failure, and
 the contract prose stated both halves of the collision on adjacent lines
 without anybody seeing it.

 The timeline types each colliding pair back to back: shift+7 then Up, then
 the apostrophe then Right, then `t` then F5. Each pair must come back as two
 DIFFERENT values, and the character half must be the CCE code point of that
 character rather than a number this test made up.

 The last two readings are the guard on the named-key space itself.
 `to-unicode key-f1` must answer 65533, the replacement character, which is
 what CCE returns for a code point it cannot represent: that is the
 assertion that named keys live outside the character space, and it fails
 the day CCE grows into them. And every printable ASCII character must have
 a CCE code point, because `key-emit` converts through `from-unicode` and a
 gap there would silently turn a typed character into negative one.

 We say:

Section: Collection

  kc-collect : List Integer -> List Integer
  kc-collect (acc) =
    let ch = poll-key
    in if ch == 0 then kc-collect acc
    else if ch == key-enter then acc
    else kc-collect (list-push acc ch)

  kc-show : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Console] Integer
  kc-show (xs) (i) (len) =
    if i >= len then 0
    else act
      print-line-uni (integer-to-text (list-at xs i))
      kc-show xs (i + 1) len
    end

Section: The Printable Range

 Walks ASCII 32 through 126 and answers the count that `from-unicode`
 refuses. It must be zero: tier 0 carries exactly the punctuation ASCII
 needs, and if that ever stops being true a typed character becomes -1 and
 this is the reading that says so.

  kc-unmapped : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  kc-unmapped (u) (acc) =
    if u > 126 then acc
    else if from-unicode u < 0 then kc-unmapped (u + 1) (acc + 1)
    else kc-unmapped (u + 1) acc

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    let ks = kc-collect []
    in act
      kc-show ks 0 (list-length ks)
      print-line-uni ("amp " & integer-to-text (char-code '&'))
      print-line-uni ("apos " & integer-to-text (char-code '\''))
      print-line-uni ("t " & integer-to-text (char-code 't'))
      print-line-uni ("named-outside-cce " & integer-to-text (to-unicode key-f1))
      print-line-uni ("unmapped-printable " & integer-to-text (kc-unmapped 32 0))
    end
  end
